# Each template is topical: it builds a coherent story around the module subject.
SCENARIO_TEMPLATES = [
"""Let me tell you about a family in {city}. They came to therapy because the matter of {tl} had turned their home into a place of silence. The father would not speak at dinner. The mother cried in the kitchen. The children learned to become invisible. When the therapist finally had all of them in one room, the truth came out gently: nobody had ever been taught how to handle {tl}, so each person had invented a private strategy. Those private strategies were colliding every day. The therapist did not scold anyone. She drew their strategies on paper, and for the first time the family saw the same picture. That picture became the beginning of a new conversation.""",

"""In {city}, a couple sat in front of a therapist and argued about {tl} for twenty minutes without pausing. Each one had a list of the other's failures. The therapist listened carefully, then asked one question: "If this problem were solved tonight, what would tomorrow morning look like in your house?" The room went quiet. The husband said, "We would eat breakfast together." The wife said, "He would ask me how I slept." Suddenly the argument was no longer about blame. It was about a lost breakfast and an unasked question. From that small picture, the therapist helped them build a plan for {tl} that felt human, not legal.""",

"""A family in {city} came to therapy because their eldest son had stopped eating with the family. The parents blamed school. The teachers blamed friends. The grandmother blamed witchcraft. The therapist asked about {tl}, and the mother began to weep. It emerged that the family had been carrying the matter of {tl} alone for two years, hiding it from relatives to protect the family name. The hiding had become heavier than the problem. When the therapist helped them speak about {tl} with one trusted aunt, the son started eating again the same week. Secrets are heavy loads, and sometimes the first intervention is simply to share the weight.""",

"""In {city}, a husband and wife came for help with {tl}. The husband was a deacon; the wife was a teacher. Both were respected in church. In public they smiled; at home they barely spoke. The therapist named this gap with kindness: "You are two good people living two separate lives." The wife cried first. The husband followed. What emerged was not scandal but exhaustion, and a shared fear of disappointing their families. Therapy for {tl} became, in practice, permission for two tired people to be honest. Honesty, in their case, was the healing.""",

"""A mother in {city} brought her teenage daughter to therapy because of {tl}. She expected the therapist to correct the girl. Instead, the therapist asked the mother to describe her own teenage years. The mother spoke for fifteen minutes about a father who travelled and a mother who was strict, and about the loneliness of being a girl in a house of rules. When she finished, she looked at her daughter and said, "I have been raising you the way I was raised, because I did not know another way." The daughter reached for her hand. The matter of {tl} did not disappear that day, but the wall between them developed a door.""",

"""In {city}, two brothers had not spoken for six years. The dispute had started over land, but it had grown into something larger involving {tl}. Their ageing mother begged them to reconcile. The therapist met each brother alone first, then brought them together. She did not ask who was right. She asked each man one question: "What do you want your mother to remember about her last years?" Both men wept. Six years of silence did not end in one session, but they agreed to share a meal with their mother. That meal became the first brick in a rebuilt road.""",

"""A family in {city} ran a successful business together and a failing home. The father managed the shop. The mother managed the accounts. The children managed their own loneliness. The presenting problem was {tl}, but the real problem was that the family had become a company with no living room. The therapist invited them to separate the two: business meetings at the shop, family time at home, no ledgers at dinner. It sounded simple. It was not. It took three months of practice before the father could sit through a meal without discussing profit. On the day he finally did, his youngest daughter said, "Daddy, you are here." He wept.""",

"""In {city}, a wife discovered that her husband had been hiding a large debt. The revelation triggered a crisis about {tl} that nearly ended the marriage. The therapist helped them do something counter-intuitive: instead of arguing about the money first, they spent a whole session on the question "What were you afraid would happen if you told me?" The husband said, "That you would see me as a failure." The wife said, "That you did not trust me." Neither of those fears was about money. Once the fears were spoken, the budget became a shared project instead of a courtroom.""",

"""A pastor in {city} came to therapy secretly, ashamed that he needed help. His marriage was cold, his children were distant, and the matter of {tl} had become a private torment. He said, "I counsel others; who counsels me?" The therapist honoured his courage and helped him see that asking for help is not the opposite of faith; it is a form of honesty. Over several months, he learned to bring the same pastoral patience home. His wife later said, "The man who came back to us was the man I married." Ministry had been consuming the minister, and therapy returned him to his own house.""",

"""In {city}, a grandmother and a daughter-in-law fought quietly for years over the raising of the children. The presenting issue was {tl}. The therapist invited both women to a session and asked each one, "What do you want the children to remember about this family?" The grandmother said, "That we were disciplined and God-fearing." The daughter-in-law said, "That we were loved and heard." The therapist wrote both sentences on the board and said, "You are not enemies. You are two generations of the same love, speaking different languages." Both women laughed through their tears, and a new arrangement began: the grandmother taught prayers, the mother taught conversation, and the children received both.""",

"""A family in {city} lost their home to a flood. In the shelter, the matter of {tl} became an unexpected battleground. The father wanted to rebuild immediately; the mother wanted to move to another town; the children wanted everything to be as it was. The therapist taught them a simple rule for the first month: "Nobody makes permanent decisions while the wound is fresh." That single rule reduced the fighting. With time, and with the help of their church and a community group, the family made decisions they could live with. Crisis reveals a family's fault lines, but it can also reveal its foundations.""",

"""In {city}, a husband was transferred to another state for work. The family had to decide whether to move together or live apart. The question of {tl} sat at the centre of every conversation. The therapist helped them do a structured decision exercise: each partner listed their fears, their hopes, their non-negotiables, and their willingness to compromise. Written down, the lists were surprisingly compatible. What had looked like an impossible conflict was actually a failure of process. They chose to move together for two years, with a review date. Decisions made in daylight are easier to live with than decisions made in fear.""",

"""A family in {city} came to therapy after a public embarrassment: a child's behaviour had been discussed in the church. The family was humiliated, and the matter of {tl} had become a family secret guarded by silence. The therapist's first task was to separate shame from responsibility. Shame says, "You are bad." Responsibility says, "Something happened, and we can respond." Over several sessions, the family learned to speak about {tl} in a way that was honest without being self-destructive. The church later became a source of support rather than judgement, because the family learned to ask for the right kind of help.""",

"""In {city}, a young couple married for two years came to therapy because of {tl}. They had expected marriage to be easy, and they were ashamed that it was not. The therapist normalised their struggle: "Every marriage has a first crisis. The crisis is not the problem. The silence around the crisis is the problem." They learned a simple weekly ritual: twenty minutes, no phones, three questions each. What went well? What was hard? What do you need from me? Within two months, the shame had lifted, and the marriage had a structure for repair.""",

"""A mother in {city} ran a small shop and raised four children alone after her husband travelled abroad and stopped sending money. The matter of {tl} was the last thing she had energy for, yet it was the thing keeping her awake. The therapist worked with her on practical load first: a support group, a cousin who could help with school runs, a church group that could help with food. Only after the practical load was lighter did they turn to {tl}. Therapy that ignores hunger and exhaustion will fail. Order of intervention is an ethical decision.""",
]

